{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## MY OWN CHANGE CREATED A VACUOUS TEST, AND A GREEN 2132-TEST SUITE HID IT\n\nFound by sweeping the repo for stale references to the refuted greenhopper route — not by any test\nfailing, which is the whole point.\n\n`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_dc_live_oracles_discriminate_5200.py` carried\n`test_dc_set_parent_DECLINES_the_non_subtask_case_by_raising`, asserting the epic case raises\n`NotImplementedError`. Change 1311 makes the epic case WRITE the Epic Link. **That test kept\npassing.**\n\n### The mechanism, because it is subtle and worth remembering\n\nThat module's client double defines only `issue()` — it has **no `fields()`**. 1311's capability\nguard (`getattr(self._client, \"fields\", None)`) therefore returns `None`, the field is\nundiscoverable, and `set_parent` declines. The assertion passes.\n\nSo the cell went green for a reason that has nothing to do with what it claims to test. Its\ndocstring says the decline happens \"because epic membership on DC is an 'Epic Link' custom field\nwritten through the Agile API under the `greenhopper` path\" — a mechanism that is now doubly wrong\n(the Agile route is refuted, and the epic case no longer declines at all). It was testing that a\ntest double is incomplete.\n\n**The guard that caused this is correct behaviour.** Without it the double would have raised\n`AttributeError` and the test would have failed loudly, alerting me. With it, the failure is\nsilent. A correct defensive behaviour masked a stale oracle — worth noting because the instinct\nwould be to blame the guard.\n\n### Why this is the epic's signature failure class, again\n\nThis is the same shape as the `08-assign` cell **this very story had to repair**: an oracle that\npasses whether or not the behaviour under test works. It is also the same shape as the five\n\"silent success\" product bugs (d067, 8d68, 751e, 2b16/88d9, 39c1). The suite reported **2132\npassed** with a dead cell inside it.\n\n### The repair, intent preserved\n\nSplit into two cells rather than deleted, because both behaviours still matter:\n\n * `test_dc_set_parent_USES_A_DIFFERENT_PATH_for_the_non_subtask_case` — the double now carries\n   `fields()` (the real client has it), and the cell asserts the epic path writes the discovered\n   Epic Link and NEVER `fields.parent`. Same intent as before: the two paths must stay\n   distinguishable.\n * `test_dc_set_parent_DECLINES_when_the_instance_has_no_epic_link_field` — the decline survives,\n   narrowed to where it is still correct. Dropping it would have lost coverage of the attributed\n   failure that keeps `dispatch_one`'s classification honest (1305).\n\nBoth mutation-checked: writing `fields.parent` instead of the Epic Link fails the first; removing\nthe decline guard fails the second.\n\n### The transferable lesson\n\n**A test double that lags the real client's interface turns a behaviour change into a silent\ngreen.** When a change makes production code call a NEW method on an injected collaborator, every\nexisting double of that collaborator becomes a candidate vacuous oracle — and none of them will\nfail to tell you. Sweeping for doubles of the changed interface belongs in the checklist next to\nsweeping for callers. This is a second-order argument for the verbosity/staleness review criterion\nfiled as [rebar:95c1-f6eb-0fa9-402e]: the stale DOCSTRING is what led me to the dead ASSERTION."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785785944026548001,"uuid":"699d726b-106f-4037-a64f-553e2b0683b8"}